{"name":"Autumn Ridge","hex":"#9b423f","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"autumn","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Traditionally the third of the four seasons, when deciduous trees lose their leaves, and temperatures and daylight hours decrease; typically regarded as spanning the months of September, October, and November in the Northern Hemisphere, and the months of March, April and May in the Southern Hemisphere.","The time period when someone or something is past its prime."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To spend the autumn (in a particular place).","To (cause to) undergo the changes associated with autumn, such as leaves changing color and falling from trees."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/autumn"},{"term":"ridge","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The back of any animal; especially the upper or projecting part of the back of a quadruped.","Any extended protuberance; a projecting line or strip."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form into a ridge.","To extend in ridges."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ridge"}],"entries":[{"title":"Autumn","blocks":[{"text":"Autumn, also known as fall in North American English, is one of the four temperate seasons on Earth. Outside the tropics, autumn marks the transition from summer to winter, beginning in September or March. Autumn is the season when the duration of daytime becomes noticeably shorter and the temperature cools considerably. Day length decreases and night length increases as the season progresses until the winter solstice in December and June. One of its main features in temperate climates is the striking change in colour of the leaves of deciduous trees as they prepare to shed."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/D%C3%BClmen%2C_Wildpark_--_2014_--_3808_color_balanced.jpg/330px-D%C3%BClmen%2C_Wildpark_--_2014_--_3808_color_balanced.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autumn","wikidata":"Q1314"},{"title":"Ridge","blocks":[{"text":"A ridge is a long, narrow, elevated geomorphologic landform, structural feature, or a combination of both separated from the surrounding terrain by steep sides. The sides of a ridge slope away from a narrow top, the crest or ridgecrest, with the terrain dropping down on either side. The crest, if narrow, is also called a ridgeline. Limitations on the dimensions of a ridge are lacking. Its height above the surrounding terrain can vary from less than a meter to hundreds of meters. A ridge can be either depositional, erosional, tectonic, or a combination of these in origin and can consist of either bedrock, loose sediment, lava, or ice depending on its origin. A ridge can occur as either an isolated, independent feature or part of a larger geomorphological and/or structural feature. Frequently, a ridge can be further subdivided into smaller geomorphic or structural elements."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/%E4%B8%AD%E6%A9%AB%E5%85%AC%E8%B7%AF%E7%9C%BA%E6%9C%9B%E6%A2%A8%E5%B1%B1%E5%8F%8A%E9%9B%AA%E5%B1%B1%E5%8D%97%E7%A8%9C2.jpg/330px-%E4%B8%AD%E6%A9%AB%E5%85%AC%E8%B7%AF%E7%9C%BA%E6%9C%9B%E6%A2%A8%E5%B1%B1%E5%8F%8A%E9%9B%AA%E5%B1%B1%E5%8D%97%E7%A8%9C2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ridge","wikidata":"Q740445"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Quebec_city%2C_Quebec%2C_Canada202205.jpg/500px-Quebec_city%2C_Quebec%2C_Canada202205.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Quebec_city%2C_Quebec%2C_Canada202205.jpg/1280px-Quebec_city%2C_Quebec%2C_Canada202205.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Quebec_city%2C_Qu